# Dastro 3.0 — manual patch

Dastro 3.0 upgrades to Astro 7. `dastro upgrade` already installed `astro@^7`,
`astro-embed@^0.13.1` and `@astrojs/alpinejs@^1` alongside dastro. The steps
below cover what the CLI cannot bump on its own.

## 1. Update the remaining local dependencies

```sh
npm update --save
```

If this fails with `ERESOLVE`, a dependency still declares a peer range for
Astro 5/6. The usual suspect is `astro-embed`: `^0.12.x` peer-depends on
`astro@^5 || ^6` only, so it blocks every Astro 7 install. Bump it explicitly
and retry:

```sh
npm i -S astro-embed@^0.13.1
```

## 2. Bump third-party Astro integrations

Integrations dastro does not declare are not touched by `dastro upgrade`. If the
upgrade had to fall back to `--legacy-peer-deps`, the tree contains packages
that do not accept Astro 7 yet. List them:

```sh
npm ls astro
```

For the boilerplate stack this usually means:

```sh
npm i -S @sentry/astro@^10.69.0 @sentry/profiling-node@^10.69.0
npm i -S datocms-structured-text-to-plain-text@^6.0.0
npm i -D concurrently@^10
```

Re-run `npm install` without `--legacy-peer-deps` afterwards to confirm the tree
resolves cleanly again.

## 3. Do not bump these yet

- **eslint 9 stack** — `eslint-plugin-astro@3` requires eslint 10.
- **typescript 6** — `astro check` and `typescript-eslint` do not support TS 7 yet.

## 4. Remove any explicit `compressHTML` from `astro.config.ts`

Astro 7 changed the default to `'jsx'`, which strips whitespace between inline
elements and breaks text spacing in rendered HTML. Dastro's integration now sets
`compressHTML: true` for you, so a project-level override is only useful if it
also sets `true` — otherwise drop it:

```diff
 export default defineConfig({
   adapter: dastroAdapterConfig(),
-  compressHTML: 'jsx',
```

## 5. Verify

```sh
npm run astro:check
npm run build
```

For anything beyond the above, see the official Astro 7 upgrade guide:
https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/upgrade-to/v7/
